  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in the Netherlands?', a: 'There is no home-education right in Dutch law. Leerplicht (5-16) is met by enrolment at a recognised school, kwalificatieplicht continues to 18, and an unrecognised online school satisfies neither. A narrow exemption exists for directional objections (Article 5 onder b, ~2,860 children in 2024-25), but it was tightened by the Hoge Raad in April 2026, enforcement is resuming, and it is essentially unavailable once a child has been enrolled. That route is a Dutch education lawyer\'s territory, and we say so plainly.' },
    { q: 'Does Smartious enrolment satisfy leerplicht?', a: 'No — and we would rather state that on page one than let a family discover it from a leerplichtambtenaar. Smartious in the Netherlands is supplementary to a school enrolment, or structured curriculum for families who already lawfully hold an exemption.' },
    { q: 'What does supplementary delivery actually look like?', a: 'A live Cambridge IGCSE-to-A-Level trajectory alongside the enrolment — real timetable, real assessment, examinations at authorised Dutch centres — scheduled after the Dutch school day on our 1-2 hour timezone offset. It is schooling-grade, not tutoring.' },
    { q: 'Is there a post-16 window like Sweden\'s?', a: 'No. Kwalificatieplicht continues to 18 until a startkwalificatie is achieved, and how it applies to internationally educated teenagers is a question for your leerplichtambtenaar or a lawyer. We will not import another country\'s rules into this one.' },
    { q: 'We already hold an Article 5b exemption — can Smartious be our curriculum?', a: 'Yes — we provide structured live curriculum to families who lawfully hold an exemption, with documented schemes of work and progress records, and without making any claims about the exemption itself. Given the 2026 legal flux, keep your own legal advice current.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare to the subsidised Dutch International Schools?', a: 'The DIS network is strong value for eligible internationally mobile families — its constraint is the queue. Smartious is the supplementary track and the waitlist bridge: immediate enrolment, live delivery, and nothing wasted when the campus place lands.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access Dutch universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Yes — both are long-established credentials assessed by Dutch universities via Studielink against programme entry requirements, and the Netherlands offers continental Europe\'s largest English-taught bachelor portfolio. UCAS and Common Application routes remain open internationally.' },
    { q: 'Where do Netherlands students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At Cambridge/Edexcel-authorised Netherlands schools accepting external candidates, deepest in the Randstad, confirmed per family per session.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the Netherlands-Nairobi time difference?', a: 'The Netherlands runs CET (UTC+1) in winter and CEST (UTC+2) in summer — only 1-2 hours behind Nairobi EAT. Supplementary classes land naturally after the Dutch school day.' },
    { q: 'Which Dutch cities does Smartious cover?', a: 'Amsterdam, The Hague, Rotterdam, Utrecht, Eindhoven/Brainport, and Groningen have dedicated pages with local market context. Live online delivery works identically for families anywhere in the Netherlands.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
